=== Lakes and Deltas ===
* '''[[Lake Zaria]]''': Located in [[Ayorin]], a significant freshwater lake supporting local agriculture and fisheries.
* '''[[Lake Keren]]''': Located in [[Nabia]], a major lake in the Kendriyan coastal region.
* '''[[Amara Delta]]''': A vast deltaic floodplain in [[Ayorin]], formed by 15 major rivers draining from the highlands into the [[Bight of Ayorin]]. Covers 149,468 km².

=== Seas and Straits ===
* '''[[Sea of Hilal]]''': A large body of water bounded by the Kaftian mainland and the island of [[Hilal]].
* '''[[Strait of Azref]]''': Northern strait connecting the Sea of Hilal to the [[Kendriyan Ocean]], 435 km wide.
* '''[[Strait of Guzo]]''': Southern strait connecting the Sea of Hilal to the [[Kendriyan Ocean]], 176 km wide at its narrowest.
* '''[[Bight of Ayorin]]''': A large bay on the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast, indenting the coastline of [[Ayorin]] and [[Tamazgha]].

== Climate ==

Southeastern Kaftia spans multiple climate zones due to its vast size and varied topography:

* '''Northern desert ([[Tamazgha]])''': {{W|Hot desert climate}} (BWh), with extreme aridity and temperature variations. Annual rainfall below 100 mm in most regions.
* '''Savanna transition zone (northern [[Enfolo]], northern [[Ayorin]], southern [[Tamazgha]])''': {{W|Tropical savanna climate}} (Aw), forming a transitional belt between the desert and the humid highlands. Distinct wet and dry seasons, with annual rainfall of 600–1,200 mm. This region is the cradle of humanity on [[Adonia]].
* '''Western delta ([[Ayorin]])''': {{W|Tropical monsoon climate}}, with heavy seasonal rainfall and extensive flooding during the monsoon season.
* '''Central highlands ([[Enfolo]])''': {{W|Subtropical highland climate}} with high rainfall, particularly on windward slopes supporting cloud forest.
* '''Southern coast ([[Jarin]], [[Itegar]], [[Orma]], [[Triphylia]])''': {{W|Mediterranean climate}}, with mild, wet winters and hot, dry summers.
* '''Eastern coast ([[Desket]], [[Nabia]])''': Dramatic climate gradient. Desket has heavy orographic rainfall on the summit plateau and arid conditions on the coastal strip. Nabia features semi-arid conditions transitioning to Mediterranean on its southern coast.

== Ecology ==

The region's ecological diversity reflects its climatic and topographic variation:

* '''Desert ecosystems''': The [[Tanaruf n-Azref]] supports drought-adapted flora and fauna including {{W|Acacia|acacia}}, {{W|Date palm|date palm}}, {{W|Fennec fox|fennec fox}}, {{W|Addax antelope|addax}}, and {{W|Dama gazelle|dama gazelle}}.
* '''Savanna ecosystems''': The savanna transition zone supports open grasslands with scattered trees, including {{W|baobab}}, {{W|acacia}}, and {{W|shea tree|shea}}. Large herbivore herds dominate the landscape.
* '''Cloud forest''': The [[Enfolo]] plateau supports a unique {{W|cloud forest}} ecosystem with moss-draped trees, tree ferns, and endemic species.
* '''Delta wetlands''': The [[Amara Delta]] supports extensive wetlands, mangrove forests, and diverse bird populations.
* '''Montane forest''': The Eastern Divide section supports dense tropical {{W|montane forest}} with high biodiversity, including forest-dwelling primates and hornbills.

== Fauna ==

The fauna of Southeastern Kaftia reflects the region's diverse ecosystems, ranging from desert-adapted species in the north to tropical forest species in the central highlands and marine life along the coasts.

=== Desert fauna ===
{{See also|Tamazgha|Tanaruf n-Azref}}
The [[Tanaruf n-Azref]] supports specialised desert-adapted species. Mammals include the {{W|fennec fox}}, the smallest canid; the {{W|addax}}, a critically endangered desert antelope; the {{W|dama gazelle}}; and the {{W|sand cat}}, a rare desert feline. The '''Kaftian sand wolf''' (''Canis kaftianus''), an endemic subspecies smaller than the grey wolf, inhabits the desert margins. Reptiles include the {{W|desert monitor}} and several species of sand viper.

Bird species include the {{W|Houbara bustard}}, a game bird historically hunted by desert nobility; the {{W|desert lark}}; and the {{W|Pharaoh eagle-owl}}. Many desert species are nocturnal or crepuscular to avoid extreme daytime temperatures.

=== Savanna fauna ===
The savanna transition zone supports the highest biomass of large mammals in Southeastern Kaftia. The open grasslands sustain vast herds of {{W|African buffalo|Kaftian buffalo}}, {{W|wildebeest}}, {{W|zebra}}, and several gazelle species. Large predators include {{W|lion}}s, {{W|leopard}}s, {{W|spotted hyena}}s, and the '''Kaftian wild dog''' (''Lycaon kaftianus''), an endangered canid distinct from the African wild dog.

The savanna is particularly notable for its megafauna, including the '''Kaftian elephant''' — the largest land mammal on the continent — and the {{W|giraffe}}. The region's fossil record indicates that it served as the evolutionary cradle of humanity, with early human remains dating to approximately 300,000 BP.

Bird species include the {{W|ostrich}}, {{W|secretary bird}}, {{W|kori bustard}}, and numerous raptors. Seasonal wetlands attract vast flocks of migratory birds, including {{W|lesser flamingo}}s and {{W|white stork}}s.

=== Cloud forest fauna ===
{{See also|Enfolo Plateau}}
The cloud forests of the [[Enfolo]] plateau support the highest biodiversity in Southeastern Kaftia. Mammals include {{W|forest elephant}}s, smaller than their savanna relatives; {{W|gorilla}}s and {{W|chimpanzee}}s; the {{W|okapi}}, a forest-dwelling giraffe relative; and the {{W|bongo (antelope)|bongo}}, a strikingly marked forest antelope. The '''Enfolo golden monkey''' (''Cercopithecus enfoloensis'') is endemic to the plateau.

Bird life is particularly diverse, including the {{W|great blue turaco}}, {{W|African grey parrot}}, and several species of {{W|hornbill}}. The '''cloud forest sunbird''', an endemic nectar-feeding species, is found only on the highest slopes.

=== Delta wetland fauna ===
{{See also|Amara Delta}}
The [[Amara Delta]] supports extensive aquatic and semi-aquatic species. {{W|Hippopotamus}}es are common in the waterways, while the {{W|West African manatee}} inhabits the delta's quieter channels. The {{W|Nile crocodile}} is the apex aquatic predator. Other mammals include the {{W|African clawless otter}} and {{W|red river hog}}.

The delta is a major bird habitat, with species including the {{W|African fish eagle}}, {{W|great white pelican}}, {{W|greater flamingo}}, and the rare {{W|shoebill}}, a prehistoric-looking stork. Fish species include the {{W|Nile perch}}, {{W|tilapia}}, and predatory {{W|tigerfish}}.

=== Montane forest fauna ===
{{See also|Eastern Divide}}
The montane forests of the Eastern Divide support several endemic species. The '''Tulluu walia''' (''Capra tulluensis'') is an endangered mountain goat endemic to the highest peaks, named after [[Tulluu Wabbii]]. Primates include the {{W|gelada}}, a grass-eating monkey, and the {{W|colobus monkey}}. The '''Kaftian highland wolf''', a rare canid related to the Ethiopian wolf, inhabits the high meadows.

Bird species include the {{W|bearded vulture}} (lammergeier), {{W|Ruppell's vulture}}, and the '''Wabbii francolin''', an endemic game bird.

=== Marine fauna ===
{{See also|Sea of Hilal|Kendriyan Ocean}}
The [[Sea of Hilal]] and [[Kendriyan Ocean]] coast support diverse marine life. The {{W|dugong}}, an endangered sea cow, inhabits seagrass beds. {{W|Indo-Pacific bottlenose dolphin}}s are common, and {{W|humpback whale}}s migrate through the region seasonally.

Sea turtles, including the {{W|green sea turtle}} and {{W|hawksbill sea turtle}}, nest on beaches throughout the Kendriyan coast. The {{W|saltwater crocodile}} inhabits coastal estuaries. Fish species include {{W|tuna}}, commercially important, and the {{W|whale shark}}, a seasonal visitor.

The island of [[Hilal]] supports massive seabird colonies, including {{W|sooty tern}}s, {{W|frigatebird}}s, and {{W|brown booby|booby}}s, whose accumulated droppings form the island's guano deposits.

=== Mediterranean coast fauna ===
The southern coast facing the [[Mesogean Sea]] supports species adapted to the Mediterranean climate. The endangered {{W|Mediterranean monk seal}} inhabits remote sea caves. {{W|Loggerhead sea turtle}}s nest on beaches. Coastal lagoons host {{W|greater flamingo}} colonies, while {{W|Eleonora's falcon}} nests on coastal cliffs.

=== Extinct species ===
Several megafauna species have been hunted to extinction in Southeastern Kaftia. The '''Mesogean pygmy elephant''', depicted in the rock art of the [[Kushish]] and [[Orma]] highlands, was driven to extinction by hunting and habitat loss during the Bronze and Iron Ages. The '''Kaftian giant buffalo''' (''Pelorovis kaftianus'') and '''desert rhinoceros''' disappeared approximately 3,000 BCE, also documented in rock art.

=== Domesticated animals ===
Several domesticated breeds are unique to Southeastern Kaftia. The '''Kaftian zebu''' is a humped cattle breed adapted to drought conditions. The '''Enfolo forest goat''' is a small, hardy breed kept by highland communities. The '''Tamazghan camel''' is a dromedary breed bred for extreme desert conditions. The '''Burawi donkey''', a sure-footed highland breed, is used throughout the southern highlands.

== Natural resources ==

Southeastern Kaftia possesses significant mineral, energy, and biological resources, distributed unevenly across the region according to geological and ecological zones.

=== Energy resources ===

==== Oil and natural gas ====
The most significant hydrocarbon deposits in Southeastern Kaftia are located in western [[Orma]], near the border with [[Kushish]] and [[Maianka]]. These fields were discovered in the mid-20th century and have since become a major component of Orma's economy. The proximity of these deposits to the now-independent states of Kushish and Maianka, formerly provinces of Burawa, has historically complicated pipeline routing and revenue-sharing negotiations.

==== Uranium ====
[[Tamazgha]] contains substantial {{W|uranium}} deposits in the desert highlands, particularly in the ''Adrar n-Tidawt'' volcanic region. These deposits are among the largest in Kaftia and represent a significant export commodity, though extraction is complicated by the remote location and extreme climate.

=== Mineral resources ===

==== Rare earth elements and coltan ====
The [[Enfolo]] plateau contains significant deposits of {{W|rare earth elements}} and {{W|coltan}} (columbite–tantalite), associated with the region's ancient volcanic geology. These strategic minerals, essential for electronics manufacturing, have attracted international mining concessions. The remote location and dense jungle terrain have limited exploitation, though extraction has increased in recent decades.

==== Iron ore and copper ====
[[Jarin]] and [[Desket]] possess substantial {{W|iron ore}} deposits. In Jarin, iron ore is extracted from the highland regions near the Enfolo border, while in Desket, deposits are associated with the escarpment geology. [[Desket]] also has significant {{W|copper}} deposits along the escarpment foothills.

==== Gold ====
Gold deposits are found in several highland regions, particularly in [[Enfolo]] and the Eastern Divide along the [[Orma]]–[[Ayorin]] border. Artisanal mining has historically occurred in these regions, with larger commercial operations developing in the modern era.

==== Phosphates and lithium ====
[[Tamazgha]] contains extensive {{W|phosphate}} deposits in the desert, as well as {{W|lithium}} salts in the evaporite basins of the ''Tazruyt n-Isli'' depression. These resources have gained increased importance with global demand for agricultural fertilisers and battery production.

=== Biological resources ===

==== Timber ====
[[Enfolo]] and [[Ayorin]] are the primary sources of timber in Southeastern Kaftia. The Enfolo plateau's cloud forests contain valuable hardwood species, while the [[Amara Delta]] in Ayorin supports mangrove and swamp forests. Timber extraction in both regions has raised environmental concerns, particularly regarding deforestation and wetland degradation.

==== Fisheries ====
The [[Bight of Ayorin]] and the [[Sea of Hilal]] support major commercial fisheries. Ayorin's fishing industry is centred on the delta and coastal waters, while [[Hilal]] and [[Desket]] also maintain significant fishing fleets. Overfishing has become a concern in some areas, leading to regional fisheries management agreements.

==== Guano ====
[[Hilal]] possesses significant {{W|guano}} deposits, accumulated by seabird colonies over millennia. These deposits have historically been used for fertiliser production, though their economic importance has declined in the modern era.

=== Resource conflicts and strategic considerations ===

The distribution of natural resources in Southeastern Kaftia has contributed to regional tensions and strategic calculations:

* '''Orma oil and gas''': The location of hydrocarbon deposits near the [[Kushish]]–[[Maianka]] border has created complex negotiations over pipeline access and transit routes to the Mesogean coast.

* '''Enfolo rare earths''': International competition for access to Enfolo's strategic minerals has led to concerns about foreign influence and resource exploitation.

* '''Triphylian Canal''': While not a natural resource, the canal's strategic position as a chokepoint for maritime trade between the [[Mesogean Sea]] and [[Kendriyan Ocean]] gives [[Triphylia]] significant economic leverage through canal tolls and transshipment fees.

